![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The `SafetyStatus::Warn` variant is defined and handled in the main loop, but `check_safety` currently only returns `SafetyStatus::Ok` or `SafetyStatus::Critical`. This makes the warning handling path dead code. Consider implementing warning thresholds (e.g., GPU temperature > 80°C or power > 320W) to utilize the warning status and provide early alerts before critical thresholds are reached. Avoid introducing simulated or mock telemetry values, ensuring that telemetry is driven only by active, real shared state.

```rust
    pub fn check_safety(telemetry: &GpuTelemetry) -> SafetyStatus {
        if telemetry.gpu_temp_c > 85.0 {
            return SafetyStatus::Critical(format!(
                "GPU thermal: {:.0}\u{00b0}C exceeds 85\u{00b0}C",
                telemetry.gpu_temp_c
            ));
        } else if telemetry.gpu_temp_c > 80.0 {
            return SafetyStatus::Warn(format!(
                "GPU thermal warning: {:.0}\u{00b0}C exceeds 80\u{00b0}C",
                telemetry.gpu_temp_c
            ));
        }
        if telemetry.power_w > 350.0 {
            return SafetyStatus::Critical(format!(
                "GPU power: {:.0}W exceeds 350W (TDP=360W)",
                telemetry.power_w
            ));
        } else if telemetry.power_w > 320.0 {
            return SafetyStatus::Warn(format!(
                "GPU power warning: {:.0}W exceeds 320W",
                telemetry.power_w
            ));
        }
        SafetyStatus::Ok
    }
```

