There is a potential safety race condition here. If the system transitions back to a `Critical` state while the asynchronous `release_task` is running in the background, the `release_task` will eventually finish and blindly set `brake_applied = false` and reset `ok_count_after_brake = 0`. This leaves the GPU unbraked and running at full power in a critical state for up to 10 steps (approx. 1 second) before the next safety check runs and re-applies the brake.

To harden this, we should check the safety status of the current telemetry immediately when the `release_task` finishes successfully. If the safety status is `Critical`, we should immediately re-spawn the `brake_task` to re-apply the emergency brake, ensuring the system fails closed.

```rust
        if release_task.as_ref().is_some_and(|task| task.is_finished()) {
            let task = release_task.take().expect("finished release task exists");
            match task.await {
                Ok(Ok(())) => {
                    brake_applied = false;
                    ok_count_after_brake = 0;
                    let (safety, _) = HardwareBridge::check_safety(&telemetry);
                    if matches!(safety, SafetyStatus::Critical(_)) {
                        brake_task = Some(tokio::task::spawn_blocking(|| {
                            HardwareBridge::apply_emergency_brake(0.5)
                        }));
                    }
                }
